data.content:
Here's the complete migration. Your code uses the **timeout enforcement** pattern — `Thread.Abort` to kill a thread that exceeds a time limit. In .NET 8, `Thread.Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(), token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException when the
            // token fires, even if work() itself doesn't check the token.
            return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

## What Changed and Why

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `new Thread(…)` + `thread.Start()` | `Task.Run(…)` | Task-based model; avoids manual thread management |
| `thread.Join(timeout)` | `task.WaitAsync(token)` | Cancellation-aware waiting (.NET 6+ API) |
| `thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter` (via constructor) | `Thread.Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` + `ResetAbort()` | Removed | No longer exists; cooperative cancellation doesn't inject exceptions |
| Manual `result`/`error` locals | Task captures return value and exceptions | `Task<T>` propagates both naturally |

## Key Pitfalls

1. **Work doesn't stop immediately.** `Task.WaitAsync` abandons the *wait*, but `work()` keeps running on the thread pool. If `work()` is a long loop, pass `CancellationToken` into it and call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at checkpoints:

   ```csharp
   // Cooperative version — work actually stops
   public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
   {
       using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
       var token = cts.Token;

       var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);

       try
       {
           return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
       }
       catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
       {
           throw new TimeoutException(
               $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
       }
   }
   ```

2. **Always dispose `CancellationTokenSource`.** It holds internal timers. The `using` statement handles this.

3. **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`.** The `when` guard ensures we only catch *our* timeout, not an unrelated cancellation.

4. **Uncooperative code (can't modify `work`).** If `work()` calls third-party code that will never check a token, `Task.WaitAsync` still lets you time out the *caller*, but the work continues in the background. For true termination of uncooperative code, move it to a child `Process` and call `Process.Kill()`.
